1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,648
Welcome to C.E.S.T.I.S Office, from C.E.S.T.I.S Technical Services.

2
00:00:04,098 --> 00:00:09,922
It's a cartoon 3D office where a team of AI coding agents works through your GitHub issues.

3
00:00:10,372 --> 00:00:17,071
Developers write the code, QA testers check every change in a real browser, and a CEO plans the work.

4
00:00:17,521 --> 00:00:21,489
You walk around, watch them work, and stay in charge of what gets merged.

5
00:00:21,939 --> 00:00:24,968
This video shows you how to use it, from start to finish.

6
00:00:25,869 --> 00:00:34,168
You need Node.js 22 or newer, git, the GitHub CLI signed in with gh auth login, and a Claude subscription.

7
00:00:34,618 --> 00:00:39,546
Clone the repository, then run npm install and npm run build.

8
00:00:39,996 --> 00:00:47,292
Try the demo first with npm run demo. It fakes GitHub and the agents, so it costs nothing and changes nothing.

9
00:00:47,742 --> 00:00:50,515
Everything in this video was recorded in the demo.

10
00:00:50,965 --> 00:00:57,237
For real work, sign Claude in once with login, check your machine with doctor, then run npm start.

11
00:00:58,138 --> 00:01:02,767
The start screen shows whether you're in demo mode. Press Enter the office to walk in.

12
00:01:03,217 --> 00:01:09,127
The first time, a short setup asks your name and introduces your CEO, and a tour shows you around.

13
00:01:09,577 --> 00:01:11,219
You can skip the tour at any time.

14
00:01:13,590 --> 00:01:19,713
You start in the lobby. Move with W, A, S and D or the arrow keys, and hold Shift to run.

15
00:01:20,163 --> 00:01:24,280
Click the view to look around with the mouse, and press Escape to let the mouse go.

16
00:01:24,730 --> 00:01:31,493
Press E, or click, to use whatever is under the crosshair: a desk, the whiteboard, the elevator or a computer.

17
00:01:31,943 --> 00:01:34,247
Press H at any time for the list of keys.

18
00:01:37,136 --> 00:01:41,637
The manager's office is yours. Your computer there is the manager's console.

19
00:01:42,087 --> 00:01:46,823
The Employee of the Month board shows whoever has helped merge the most pull requests.

20
00:01:47,273 --> 00:01:55,103
Across the lobby is the CEO's corner office, and the waiting room, where job candidates wait in their own clothes until they're hired.

21
00:01:57,265 --> 00:02:04,518
On the console's Floors tab you connect a GitHub repository or one of your project folders, or start a new blank repo.

22
00:02:04,968 --> 00:02:12,520
Each project becomes a floor of the building. Turn on auto-assign, and free developers pick up backlog issues by themselves.

23
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:19,562
Team is where you hire developers and QA testers, rename them, and choose each one's coding agent and model.

24
00:02:20,012 --> 00:02:27,671
Settings holds the office-wide choices: the default model, a limit on how many agents work at once, and how hiring works.

25
00:02:28,572 --> 00:02:32,945
Press P anywhere to take out your phone. This is how you talk to your CEO.

26
00:02:33,395 --> 00:02:41,054
The CEO studies each project, writes its QA checklist, files the work as GitHub issues, and proposes who to hire.

27
00:02:41,504 --> 00:02:45,771
Proposals wait in Hires. Read the resume, then approve or decline.

28
00:02:46,221 --> 00:02:51,597
Company shows the whole business at a glance: what needs you, and how each project is doing.

29
00:02:52,047 --> 00:03:01,860
This week shows productivity: pull requests merged, the time from issue to merge, QA's pass rate, cost per merged pull request, and how busy the staff are.

30
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:07,069
Each floor is one project. Ride the elevator, or press Go on the phone.

31
00:03:07,519 --> 00:03:11,466
Your team works at their desks in C.E.S.T.I.S staff shirts and name badges.

32
00:03:11,916 --> 00:03:14,881
Press Tab to see who is working right now, and what on.

33
00:03:25,209 --> 00:03:28,836
Walk up to a desk and press E to open that agent's terminal.

34
00:03:29,286 --> 00:03:35,387
Every agent is a real coding agent running on your machine, in its own copy of the repository.

35
00:03:35,837 --> 00:03:39,186
Watch it work live, or type into it to give it instructions.

36
00:03:39,636 --> 00:03:47,039
If an agent goes quiet for twenty minutes your phone tells you. After thirty, the office stops it and puts the work back in the queue.

37
00:03:47,939 --> 00:03:51,182
The whiteboard is the Kanban board. Press E in front of it.

38
00:03:51,632 --> 00:03:57,136
Issues move from the backlog, to in progress, to QA, to ready to merge, and finally to merged.

39
00:03:57,586 --> 00:04:00,764
You can file new issues here, and assign them to a developer.

40
00:04:01,664 --> 00:04:04,053
Every pull request goes to a QA tester.

41
00:04:04,503 --> 00:04:13,207
They review the code, run the tests and the build, and try the change in a real browser, then post a report with screenshots on the pull request.

42
00:04:13,657 --> 00:04:18,159
If QA fails it, the developer fixes it, and QA re-tests what changed.

43
00:04:19,058 --> 00:04:22,578
When QA passes, nothing merges until you've checked its work.

44
00:04:23,028 --> 00:04:28,703
Press Approve QA to read QA's summary and every check it made, with a link to the full report.

45
00:04:29,153 --> 00:04:35,019
Once you approve, the office merges it as soon as GitHub's checks are green, and everyone celebrates.

46
00:04:35,919 --> 00:04:43,556
Press B for the staff room: everyone's ID card, their shirt, what they're working on, and how many pull requests they've helped merge.

47
00:04:44,006 --> 00:04:48,017
Look at someone and press V for a high five, or C to change their shirt.

48
00:04:48,467 --> 00:04:51,091
And press J for an office jam, with confetti.

49
00:04:51,993 --> 00:05:00,910
That's C.E.S.T.I.S Office: connect a repository, let the CEO plan the work, approve the hires, and check QA's reports before anything merges.

50
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:05,520
The README and the How it works guide have all the details. Happy building!
